\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Spatial symmetries and charge conjugation}
\date{2026-11-11}

\p{For the \vocabk{pure-gauge transfer operator}{fdip-007Z},
 a signed graph automorphism #{a} permutes vertices and oriented
 edges, sending each edge to a chosen edge or its reverse.
 Its action #{A_a} on configurations is specified by
##{(A_aU)_{a(e)}=U_e^{\epsilon_e},\qquad\epsilon_e\in\{1,-1\},}
where #{\epsilon_e} records that orientation choice. Suppose
 #{V(A_aU)=V(U)}. The inverse-pullback unitary
 #{R_af=f\circ A_a^{-1}} preserves the physical space,
 commutes with #{T} and fixes the positive vacuum #{\Omega}.}

\p{For #{G=SU(N)}, #{N\ge2}, the same conclusions hold for
 charge conjugation #{CU=(\overline{U_e})_e} whenever
 #{V(CU)=V(U)}. No additional assumption on the admitted
 central inversion-symmetric kernel is needed.
 If a bounded real gauge-invariant function #{F} is invariant
 under a collection of these symmetries, its centered vector
 #{(M_F-\langle\Omega,M_F\Omega\rangle I)\Omega}
 and all its spectral components belong to their common
 fixed subspace.}

\proof{
\p{Product Haar probability is invariant under edge permutation
 and inversion. The graph automorphism intertwines vertex gauge
 actions with the corresponding vertex permutation, so its
 pullback preserves the physical space. A simultaneous inversion
 of two edge variables replaces #{k(xy^{-1})} by #{k(x^{-1}y)}.
 Centrality and inversion symmetry give
##{k(x^{-1}y)=k(yx^{-1})=k((xy^{-1})^{-1})=k(xy^{-1}).}
Thus #{c(A_aU,A_aU')=c(U,U')}; the assumed potential
 invariance gives the same identity for #{K_T}. Changing
 variables in the integral proves #{R_aT=TR_a}.}

\p{For #{g\in SU(N)}, unitary diagonalization shows that
 #{\overline g} and #{g^{-1}} have the same eigenvalues
 with multiplicities and are conjugate in #{SU(N)}. A unitary
 conjugator can be multiplied by a scalar to have determinant
 one. Therefore
##{k(\overline g)=k(g^{-1})=k(g).}
Complex conjugation is a Haar-preserving group automorphism
 and intertwines gauge actions by #{h\mapsto\overline h}.
 The kernel and potential identities again prove commutation
 and physical-space preservation.}

\p{Each pullback sends #{\Omega} to a positive normalized top
 eigenvector, so \ref{fdip-00N6} makes it equal to #{\Omega}.
 Also #{R_aM_FR_a^{-1}=M_{F\circ A_a^{-1}}}, with the
 analogous identity for #{C}. An invariant #{F} therefore
 gives a fixed centered vector. Commutation with the compact
 self-adjoint transfer makes every eigenspace invariant and its
 orthogonal spectral projection commute with these unitaries.
 This proves the last assertion.}
\p{The metric used in the [SU(2) and SU(3) heat examples](fdip-00MM)
 extends to #{\mathfrak{su}(N)} as
 #{\langle X,Y\rangle=-\operatorname{tr}(XY)}.
 It is invariant under conjugation of entries because the trace
 is real on this real Lie algebra. More generally, an
 Ad-invariant positive inner product on this simple Lie algebra
 is a positive scalar multiple of that one: its representing
 positive self-adjoint map commutes with the adjoint action,
 and each eigenspace is an ideal, forcing a single eigenvalue.
 Thus complex conjugation preserves every such metric and its
 heat kernel. Other groups or automorphisms require their own
 metric-invariance check.}
}
